%% Part II: Programming Language Design

%% ============================================================
%% 19.1 Introduction
%% ============================================================
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:modules-intro}

As programs grow beyond a few hundred lines, the need for
\emph{modularization} becomes paramount.  Modules provide the mechanism for
organizing large programs into separate, composable units with controlled
interfaces.  A good module system enables independent development, separate
compilation, information hiding, and code reuse.

This chapter surveys the module systems of modern programming languages,
with particular emphasis on the ML module system---the most influential and
mathematically elegant module system in existence.  We examine functors and
parameterized modules, type classes and type modules, namespace management,
separate compilation, and the formal semantics of modules.

\begin{definition}[Module]\label{def:module-intro}
  A \emph{module} is a program unit that bundles related declarations
  (types, values, functions, sub-modules) and controls their visibility
  through an \emph{interface} (signature, export list).  Modules are the
  primary mechanism for structuring large programs.
\end{definition}

%% ============================================================
%% 19.2 The ML Module System
%% ============================================================
\section{The ML Module System}\label{sec:ml-modules}

The ML module system, designed by MacQueen~\cite{macqueen1984modules}, is
the gold standard for module systems.  It provides three key concepts:
\emph{structures} (modules), \emph{signatures} (interfaces), and
\emph{functors} (module-level functions).

\subsection{Structures}\label{sec:structures}

\begin{definition}[Structure]\label{def:structure}
  A \emph{structure} is a collection of related declarations:
  \begin{verbatim}
  structure Stack = struct
    type 'a t
    val empty : 'a t
    val push : 'a * 'a t -> 'a t
    val pop : 'a t -> 'a * 'a t
    val is_empty : 'a t -> bool
  end
  \end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Signatures}\label{sec:signatures}

\begin{definition}[Signature]\label{def:signature}
  A \emph{signature} is a type for structures---it specifies the interface
  without the implementation:
  \begin{verbatim}
  signature STACK = sig
    type 'a t
    val empty : 'a t
    val push : 'a * 'a t -> 'a t
    val pop : 'a t -> 'a * 'a t
    val is_empty : 'a t -> bool
  end
  \end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

A structure can be \emph{ascribed} to a signature, which restricts its
visible interface:
\begin{verbatim}
  structure Stack :> STACK = struct ... end
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Opaque vs. Transparent Ascription}\label{sec:ascription}

\begin{definition}[Ascription]\label{def:ascription}
  \emph{Ascription} constrains a structure to match a signature:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Transparent} ($:=$): The type components remain known.
    \item \textbf{Opaque} ($:> $): The type components are hidden (abstract).
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Opaque Ascription]\label{ex:opaque-ascription}
  \begin{verbatim}
  structure AbsStack :> STACK = struct
    type 'a t = 'a list
    val empty = []
    fun push (x, s) = x :: s
    fun pop (x :: s) = (x, s)
    fun is_empty [] = true
      | is_empty _ = false
  end
  \end{verbatim}
  Outside the module, the type \texttt{AbsStack.t} is abstract---clients
  cannot assume it is a list.
\end{example}

\subsection{Functors}\label{sec:functors}

\begin{definition}[Functor]\label{def:functor}
  A \emph{functor} is a function from structures to structures.  It
  parameterizes a module over another module:
  \begin{verbatim}
  functor MakeSet (Elem : ORDERED) :> SET = struct
    type elem = Elem.t
    type t = elem list
    (* ... implementation ... *)
  end
  \end{verbatim}
\end{definition}

Functors enable \emph{generic programming at the module level}: a single
implementation can be specialized to different types by applying the functor
to different argument structures.

\begin{example}[Functor Application]\label{ex:functor-app}
  \begin{verbatim}
  structure IntOrdered = struct
    type t = int
    val compare = Int.compare
  end
  
  structure IntSet = MakeSet(IntOrdered)
  (* IntSet is a set of integers *)
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

%% ============================================================
%% 19.3 Haskell Modules and Type Classes
%% ============================================================
\section{Haskell Modules and Type Classes}\label{sec:haskell-modules}

\subsection{Haskell Module System}\label{sec:haskell-module-system}

Haskell's module system is simpler than ML's but provides similar
functionality:

\begin{verbatim}
module Data.Set (Set, empty, insert, member, delete) where
  
  data Set a = ...
  
  empty :: Set a
  insert :: Ord a => a -> Set a -> Set a
  member :: Ord a => a -> Set a -> Bool
  delete :: Ord a => a -> Set a -> Set a
\end{verbatim}

Key features:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Export lists}: Explicitly list exported names.
  \item \textbf{Qualified imports}: \texttt{import qualified Data.Map as M}.
  \item \textbf{No functors}: Parameterization is through type classes.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Type Classes as Module System}\label{sec:type-classes-modules}

\begin{definition}[Type Class]\label{def:type-class}
  A \emph{type class} defines a set of operations that types can implement.
  It is analogous to an ML signature, and a type class instance is analogous
  to a structure matching a signature.
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Type Class as Module]\label{ex:type-class-module}
  \begin{verbatim}
  class Eq a where
    (==) :: a -> a -> Bool
    (/=) :: a -> a -> Bool
  
  instance Eq Int where
    x == y = primEqInt x y
    x /= y = not (x == y)
  \end{verbatim}
  The \texttt{Eq} class is like a signature; the \texttt{instance Eq Int}
  is like a structure.
\end{example}

The key difference from ML modules is that type classes support
\emph{ad hoc polymorphism}: different types can implement the same class
with different behavior.  ML functors achieve the same effect through
explicit parameterization.

\subsection{Comparison: ML Modules vs. Haskell Type Classes}\label{sec:ml-vs-haskell}

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{ML Modules vs. Haskell Type Classes}
  \label{tab:ml-vs-haskell}
  \begin{tabular}{lll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Feature} & \textbf{ML Modules} & \textbf{Haskell Type Classes} \\
    \hline
    Interface & Signature & Type class \\
    Implementation & Structure & Instance \\
    Parameterization & Functor & Context (\texttt{=>}) \\
    First-class & Yes (as values) & No (implicit) \\
    Type abstraction & Opaque ascription & Existential types \\
    Multiple instances & Via different functors & Via newtype \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

%% ============================================================
%% 19.4 OCaml Module Extensions
%% ============================================================
\section{OCaml Module Extensions}\label{sec:ocaml-modules}

OCaml extends the ML module system with several features:

\subsection{First-Class Modules}\label{sec:first-class-modules}

\begin{verbatim}
  (* Pack a module as a value *)
  let module_value = (module IntSet : SET)
  
  (* Unpack a module from a value *)
  let module M = (val module_value : SET) in
  M.empty
\end{verbatim}

First-class modules allow modules to be stored in data structures, passed
as arguments to ordinary functions, and returned as results.

\subsection{Module Type Inclusion}\label{sec:module-inclusion}

\begin{verbatim}
  module type EXTENDED_STACK = sig
    include STACK
    val append : 'a t -> 'a t -> 'a t
    val map : ('a -> 'b) -> 'a t -> 'b t
  end
\end{verbatim}

The \texttt{include} directive copies all declarations from the included
signature, enabling signature extension.

\subsection{Applicative vs. Generative Functors}\label{sec:applicative-generative}

\begin{definition}[Applicative Functor]\label{def:applicative-functor}
  An \emph{applicative} functor produces the same result type when applied
  to the same argument type.  \texttt{F(A)} and \texttt{F(A)} are
  compatible types.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Generative Functor]\label{def:generative-functor}
  A \emph{generative} functor produces a fresh type each time it is
  applied.  \texttt{F(A)} and \texttt{F(A)} are \emph{incompatible} types.
\end{definition}

OCaml uses applicative functors by default; SML/NJ uses generative
functors.  The choice affects whether structures created by the same functor
application can be used interchangeably.

%% ============================================================
%% 19.5 Rust's Module System
%% ============================================================
\section{Rust's Module System}\label{sec:rust-modules}

\subsection{Crate and Module Structure}\label{sec:rust-crate}

Rust organizes code into \emph{crates} (compilation units) and
\emph{modules} (namespaces within a crate):

\begin{verbatim}
  // lib.rs
  pub mod collections {
      pub mod hash_map {
          pub struct HashMap<K, V> { ... }
          impl<K, V> HashMap<K, V> { ... }
      }
      pub mod btree_map {
          pub struct BTreeMap<K, V> { ... }
      }
  }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{Visibility Rules}\label{sec:rust-visibility}

Rust has fine-grained visibility control:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \texttt{pub}: Visible everywhere.
  \item \texttt{pub(crate)}: Visible within the crate.
  \item \texttt{pub(super)}: Visible in the parent module.
  \item \texttt{pub(in path)}: Visible within the specified path.
  \item Private (default): Visible only within the current module.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Traits as Interfaces}\label{sec:rust-traits}

Rust's \emph{traits} are analogous to type classes:

\begin{verbatim}
  pub trait Iterator {
      type Item;
      fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item>;
  }
  
  impl<T> Iterator for Vec<T> {
      type Item = T;
      fn next(&mut self) -> Option<T> { ... }
  }
\end{verbatim}

Traits provide ad hoc polymorphism with static dispatch (monomorphization)
or dynamic dispatch (trait objects).

%% ============================================================
%% 19.6 Namespace Management
%% ============================================================
\section{Namespace Management}\label{sec:namespace-management}

\subsection{Import and Export}\label{sec:import-export}

Different languages provide different mechanisms for managing namespaces:

\begin{table}[htbp]
  \centering
  \caption{Import Mechanisms}
  \label{tab:import-mechanisms}
  \begin{tabular}{ll}
    \hline
    \textbf{Language} & \textbf{Import Syntax} \\
    \hline
    Python & \texttt{from module import name} \\
    Java & \texttt{import package.Class;} \\
    Rust & \texttt{use crate::module::name;} \\
    Haskell & \texttt{import qualified Module as M} \\
    ML & \texttt{open Structure} \\
    C++ & \texttt{\#include} / \texttt{import} (C++20) \\
    \hline
  \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Name Conflicts}\label{sec:name-conflicts}

When two modules export names with the same identifier, the import
mechanism must resolve the conflict:

\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Qualified access}: Require the module prefix
    (\texttt{M.x} vs.\ \texttt{N.x}).
  \item \textbf{Selective import}: Import only specific names.
  \item \textbf{Renaming}: Import with a different local name.
  \item \textbf{Shadowing}: The later import shadows the earlier one.
\end{itemize}

\begin{example}[Name Conflict Resolution]\label{ex:name-conflict}
  \textbf{Rust}:
  \begin{verbatim}
  use std::io::Result;          // imports Result
  use mylib::Result as MyResult; // imports with rename
  \end{verbatim}
  
  \textbf{Python}:
  \begin{verbatim}
  from module_a import process
  from module_b import process as process_b
  \end{verbatim}
\end{example}

% Annotated walkthrough
\subsection{Resolving Re-Exports by Declaration Identity}

Two imports of a spelling may reach the same declaration. If a
resolver diagnoses ambiguity by counting paths, it may reject such
a diamond even though no choice of declaration is at stake.
Assume an acyclic graph of modules, each with a table of public
local declarations and zero or more wildcard public re-exports.
Declaration identifiers are globally unique and stable within the
compilation. There are no renames, and local public declarations
take precedence over re-exports. To resolve one spelling, compute
the set of reachable identifiers under those rules. An empty set
means absent, a singleton succeeds, and a larger set is ambiguous.
The cache is private to the query for that spelling.

\begin{sudocode}[Collect public origins of a spelling]
\Function{Origins}{$m,n$}
  \If{$n$ is public and local in $m$}
    \State \Return $\{\mathrm{decl}(m,n)\}$ \Comment{Local wins}
  \EndIf
  \If{$m$ is cached}
    \State \Return cached set for $m$
  \EndIf
  \State $R\gets\varnothing$
  \ForAll{modules $p$ publicly re-exported by $m$}
    \State $R\gets R\cup\Call{Origins}{p,n}$
    \Comment{Union by identity, not path}
  \EndFor
  \State Cache $R$ for $m$
  \State \Return $R$
\EndFunction
\end{sudocode}

\paragraph{Worked trace.}
\texttt{Core} declares \texttt{size} with identity $10$.
\texttt{Left} and \texttt{Right} each re-export \texttt{Core},
and \texttt{Top} re-exports both. Resolving \texttt{size} from
\texttt{Top}, the left traversal finds $\{10\}$; the right
traversal finds the same singleton. The union $\{10\}$ resolves
successfully. If \texttt{Right} now declares its own public
\texttt{size} with identity $23$, its local rule produces
$\{23\}$ without descending. The union at \texttt{Top} is
$\{10,23\}$, a genuine conflict. Reporting one path to each
identity makes this diagnostic actionable.

\paragraph{Invariant and scope.}
Every cached set is complete under the chosen visibility and
precedence rules, by induction in reverse dependency order.
Resolving only the first match would make the outcome depend on
import traversal order. Cyclic re-exports require a separate
policy, such as rejecting cycles or defining a fixed point;
memoization of completed entries alone does not handle them.
Nor does this deliberately small example prescribe a particular
language's treatment of private or renamed declarations.

\subsection{Open vs. Closed Namespaces}\label{sec:open-closed}

\begin{definition}[Open Namespace]\label{def:open-namespace}
  An \emph{open} namespace (e.g., ML's \texttt{open}, C++'s \texttt{using
  namespace}) brings all names from a module into the current scope.  This
  is convenient but can cause name conflicts.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Closed Namespace]\label{def:closed-namespace}
  A \emph{closed} namespace requires explicit qualification for all
  imported names.  This is verbose but avoids conflicts.
\end{definition}

Most modern languages favor closed namespaces with selective imports,
reflecting the principle that explicit is better than implicit.

%% ============================================================
%% 19.7 Separate Compilation and Linking
%% ============================================================
\section{Separate Compilation and Linking}\label{sec:separate-compilation}

\subsection{The Separate Compilation Problem}\label{sec:separate-compilation-problem}

\begin{definition}[Separate Compilation]\label{def:separate-compilation}
  \emph{Separate compilation} is the ability to compile modules
  independently and link them together to form a complete program.  This
  requires:
  \begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Interface files}: Each module exports an interface that
      other modules can depend on.
    \item \textbf{Dependency tracking}: The build system knows which modules
      depend on which.
    \item \textbf{Binary compatibility}: Compiled modules can be linked
      without recompilation.
  \end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{Approaches to Separate Compilation}\label{sec:separate-compilation-approaches}

\subsubsection{Header Files (C/C++)}

C and C++ use a textual inclusion model:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Header files} (\texttt{.h}): Declare interfaces.
  \item \textbf{Source files} (\texttt{.c}, \texttt{.cpp}): Define
    implementations.
  \item \textbf{Preprocessor inclusion}: \texttt{\#include} copies header
    text into source files.
\end{itemize}

This approach is simple but fragile: header files must be kept in sync with
implementations, and changes to headers require recompilation of all
dependents.

\subsubsection{Module Interfaces (ML, Haskell, Rust)}

Modern languages use compiled interface files:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{ML}: \texttt{.cmi} files (compiled signatures).
  \item \textbf{Haskell}: \texttt{.hi} files (interface files).
  \item \textbf{Rust}: \texttt{.rmeta} files (metadata).
  \item \textbf{Java}: \texttt{.class} files (bytecode, which includes
    type information).
\end{itemize}

\subsubsection{C++20 Modules}

C++20 introduces a module system that replaces header files:
\begin{verbatim}
  // math.cppm (module interface)
  export module math;
  export int add(int a, int b) { return a + b; }
  
  // main.cpp (consumer)
  import math;
  int main() { return add(1, 2); }
\end{verbatim}

\subsection{The Linking Problem}\label{sec:linking}

\begin{definition}[Linking]\label{def:linking}
  \emph{Linking} is the process of combining separately compiled modules
  into a single executable.  The linker resolves external references
  (symbols used but not defined in a module) to their definitions.
\end{definition}

Linking involves:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{Symbol resolution}: Matching references to definitions.
  \item \textbf{Relocation}: Adjusting addresses for the final memory layout.
  \item \textbf{Library search}: Finding libraries that provide needed
    symbols.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{One Definition Rule}\label{sec:odr}

\begin{definition}[ODR]\label{def:odr}
  The \emph{One Definition Rule} (C++) states that a name can have at most
  one definition in a program (with some exceptions for inline functions
  and templates).  Violating the ODR leads to undefined behavior.
\end{definition}

Module systems help enforce the ODR by ensuring that each module is
compiled exactly once and that its interface is consistent across all uses.

%% ============================================================
%% 19.8 Formal Semantics of Modules
%% ============================================================
\section{Formal Semantics of Modules}\label{sec:formal-modules}

\subsection{The Typed Module Calculus}\label{sec:module-calculus}

The semantics of ML-style modules can be formalized using a module calculus
that extends the typed lambda calculus with module-level constructs:

\begin{grammar}{Module Calculus}
  \production{\sigma}{\mathbf{sig}\ \{ \vec{d} \}} \\
  \production{M}{\mathbf{struct}\ \{ \vec{d} \} \alt M : \sigma \alt F(M) \alt X}
\end{grammar}

where $\vec{d}$ ranges over declarations (value bindings, type bindings,
sub-module bindings).

\subsection{Elaboration}\label{sec:elaboration}

\begin{definition}[Elaboration]\label{def:elaboration}
  \emph{Elaboration} is the process of translating module-level constructs
  into core language constructs.  For example:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item A structure becomes a tuple of its components.
    \item A functor becomes a function from tuples to tuples.
    \item Signature ascription becomes existential type packing.
  \end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{example}[Elaboration of a Functor]\label{ex:elaboration-functor}
  \begin{verbatim}
  functor F (X : SIG) = struct val y = X.x + 1 end
  \end{verbatim}
  Elaborates to:
  \[
    F = \lambda (X : \exists \alpha.\, \{ x : \alpha \}).\, \{ y = X.x + 1 \}
  \]
  The signature \texttt{SIG} becomes an existential type.
\end{example}

\subsection{Type Checking Modules}\label{sec:type-checking-modules}

Module type checking involves:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item \textbf{Signature matching}: Checking that a structure provides all
    the components required by a signature.
  \item \textbf{Type realization}: Matching abstract types in the signature
    with concrete types in the structure.
  \item \textbf{Functor application}: Checking that the argument matches
    the functor's parameter signature.
\end{enumerate}

\begin{typerules}{Module Typing (Selected)}
  \typerule{\textsc{Struct}}{
    \Gamma \vdash d_1 : \sigma_1 \quad \ldots \quad \Gamma \vdash d_n : \sigma_n
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash \struct\ \{ d_1; \ldots; d_n \} : \sig\ \{ \sigma_1; \ldots; \sigma_n \}
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Ascribe}}{
    \Gamma \vdash M : \sigma' \quad \sigma' \texttt{ matches } \sigma
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash M : \sigma : \sigma
  } \\[8pt]
  \typerule{\textsc{Apply}}{
    \Gamma \vdash F : \sigma_1 \to \sigma_2 \quad
    \Gamma \vdash M : \sigma_1
  }{
    \Gamma \vdash F(M) : \sigma_2
  }
\end{typerules}

%% ============================================================
%% 19.9 Summary
%% ============================================================
\section{Summary}\label{sec:modules-summary}

Module systems are essential for structuring large programs.  The ML module
system---with structures, signatures, and functors---provides the most
complete and mathematically elegant design.  Haskell's type classes offer a
different approach, trading first-class modules for implicit resolution and
ad hoc polymorphism.  Rust's module system combines file-based organization
with trait-based interfaces.

The key design decisions in module systems are:
\begin{itemize}
  \item \textbf{First-class vs. second-class}: Can modules be passed as
    values?
  \item \textbf{Applicative vs. generative}: Do functor applications
    produce the same or different types?
  \item \textbf{Open vs. sealed}: Are type components visible or abstract?
  \item \textbf{Separate compilation}: How are interfaces represented for
    independent compilation?
\end{itemize}

Each choice has implications for expressiveness, performance, and ease of
use.  The ``best'' module system depends on the language's goals and
application domain.
